1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,114
Ding-dong.

2
00:00:01,294 --> 00:00:04,719
It's a Tuesday afternoon in nineteen fifty-seven.

3
00:00:04,899 --> 00:00:06,836
Your mother wipes her hands on her

4
00:00:06,836 --> 00:00:08,603
apron and opens the front door.

5
00:00:08,783 --> 00:00:11,049
Standing on the porch is a man

6
00:00:11,049 --> 00:00:12,937
in a neat suit and a hat,

7
00:00:12,937 --> 00:00:14,901
holding a big sample case.

8
00:00:15,081 --> 00:00:17,178
He smiles, tips his hat,

9
00:00:17,178 --> 00:00:19,363
and says, good afternoon,

10
00:00:19,363 --> 00:00:19,888
ma'am.

11
00:00:20,068 --> 00:00:21,972
I'm from the Fuller Brush Company.

12
00:00:22,152 --> 00:00:23,138
May I come in?

13
00:00:23,488 --> 00:00:25,865
For most of the twentieth century,

14
00:00:25,865 --> 00:00:29,011
the door-to-door salesman was a familiar part

15
00:00:29,011 --> 00:00:30,199
of American life.

16
00:00:30,379 --> 00:00:31,807
The Fuller Brush man.

17
00:00:31,987 --> 00:00:32,997
The Avon lady.

18
00:00:33,177 --> 00:00:35,348
The encyclopedia salesman.

19
00:00:35,528 --> 00:00:37,850
The vacuum cleaner man who poured dirt

20
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:39,989
on your carpet to prove his machine

21
00:00:39,989 --> 00:00:41,089
could clean it up.

22
00:00:41,269 --> 00:00:43,669
They knocked on millions of doors every

23
00:00:43,669 --> 00:00:44,346
single day.

24
00:00:44,695 --> 00:00:47,401
Today, they've almost completely vanished.

25
00:00:47,581 --> 00:00:50,564
So what happened to the door-to-door salesman?

26
00:00:50,744 --> 00:00:52,065
Let's meet them,

27
00:00:52,065 --> 00:00:52,973
one by one.

28
00:00:53,153 --> 00:00:54,241
Who they were,

29
00:00:54,241 --> 00:00:55,407
what they sold,

30
00:00:55,407 --> 00:00:56,650
how they did it,

31
00:00:56,650 --> 00:00:57,894
and why one day,

32
00:00:57,894 --> 00:00:59,992
they simply stopped coming.

33
00:01:00,341 --> 00:01:03,802
Door-to-door selling is as old as America

34
00:01:03,802 --> 00:01:04,393
itself.

35
00:01:04,573 --> 00:01:06,474
In the eighteen hundreds,

36
00:01:06,474 --> 00:01:09,666
Yankee peddlers walked the back roads with

37
00:01:09,666 --> 00:01:11,262
packs on their backs,

38
00:01:11,262 --> 00:01:12,707
selling pots, pans,

39
00:01:12,707 --> 00:01:15,595
needles and cloth to farm families who

40
00:01:15,595 --> 00:01:17,495
lived far from any store.

41
00:01:17,675 --> 00:01:19,602
Some traveled by wagon.

42
00:01:19,782 --> 00:01:21,355
For isolated families,

43
00:01:21,355 --> 00:01:23,857
the peddler brought not just goods,

44
00:01:23,857 --> 00:01:26,145
but news from the outside world.

45
00:01:26,494 --> 00:01:28,647
The most famous of them all was

46
00:01:28,647 --> 00:01:30,105
the Fuller Brush man.

47
00:01:30,285 --> 00:01:31,814
In nineteen oh six,

48
00:01:31,814 --> 00:01:34,549
a young man from Nova Scotia named

49
00:01:34,549 --> 00:01:37,847
Alfred Fuller started making brushes in a

50
00:01:37,847 --> 00:01:39,537
basement in Hartford,

51
00:01:39,537 --> 00:01:40,502
Connecticut.

52
00:01:40,682 --> 00:01:42,258
He made them at night and sold

53
00:01:42,258 --> 00:01:43,991
them door to door during the day.

54
00:01:44,171 --> 00:01:46,588
His brushes were better than anything in

55
00:01:46,588 --> 00:01:48,884
the stores, and housewives loved them.

56
00:01:49,234 --> 00:01:51,575
Fuller hired thousands of salesmen,

57
00:01:51,575 --> 00:01:54,718
called dealers, who were independent and worked

58
00:01:54,718 --> 00:01:55,655
on commission.

59
00:01:55,835 --> 00:01:57,749
By the late nineteen forties,

60
00:01:57,749 --> 00:02:00,455
the company had thousands of Fuller Brush

61
00:02:00,455 --> 00:02:01,709
men across America.

62
00:02:01,889 --> 00:02:03,978
They sold scrub brushes,

63
00:02:03,978 --> 00:02:06,240
hairbrushes, mops, brooms,

64
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:09,285
cleaning products and toothbrushes.

65
00:02:09,465 --> 00:02:11,323
Each one came with a guarantee.

66
00:02:11,672 --> 00:02:14,135
The Fuller Brush man was famous for

67
00:02:14,135 --> 00:02:15,121
his free gift.

68
00:02:15,301 --> 00:02:17,772
Before he even started his pitch,

69
00:02:17,772 --> 00:02:20,917
he handed the housewife a little vegetable

70
00:02:20,917 --> 00:02:22,789
brush, or a small sample.

71
00:02:22,969 --> 00:02:24,327
It was a clever trick.

72
00:02:24,507 --> 00:02:26,341
Once you'd accepted a gift,

73
00:02:26,341 --> 00:02:28,107
it was harder to say no to

74
00:02:28,107 --> 00:02:28,583
a sale.

75
00:02:28,763 --> 00:02:31,326
Millions of American kitchens had a free

76
00:02:31,326 --> 00:02:33,441
Fuller brush hanging by the sink.

77
00:02:33,791 --> 00:02:36,714
The Fuller Brush man became an American

78
00:02:36,714 --> 00:02:37,089
icon.

79
00:02:37,269 --> 00:02:39,208
Comic strips joked about him.

80
00:02:39,388 --> 00:02:40,995
In nineteen forty-eight,

81
00:02:40,995 --> 00:02:43,271
Red Skelton starred in a hit movie

82
00:02:43,271 --> 00:02:45,146
called The Fuller Brush Man.

83
00:02:45,326 --> 00:02:46,975
And the company's slogan,

84
00:02:46,975 --> 00:02:48,954
which every dealer was taught,

85
00:02:48,954 --> 00:02:49,680
was simple.

86
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:51,857
Always leave them smiling.

87
00:02:52,206 --> 00:02:54,798
Being a Fuller Brush man was hard

88
00:02:54,798 --> 00:02:55,190
work.

89
00:02:55,370 --> 00:02:57,781
Dealers walked miles every day,

90
00:02:57,781 --> 00:03:01,125
climbing porch steps and knocking on dozens

91
00:03:01,125 --> 00:03:01,825
of doors.

92
00:03:02,005 --> 00:03:03,933
Many were turned away at most of

93
00:03:03,933 --> 00:03:04,235
them.

94
00:03:04,415 --> 00:03:06,502
But a good dealer could earn a

95
00:03:06,502 --> 00:03:09,147
decent living, and for many young men,

96
00:03:09,147 --> 00:03:12,627
including some who later became famous businessmen

97
00:03:12,627 --> 00:03:15,271
and politicians, it was the first real

98
00:03:15,271 --> 00:03:16,524
job they ever had.

99
00:03:16,873 --> 00:03:18,429
Then there was the Avon lady.

100
00:03:18,609 --> 00:03:21,527
Avon began in eighteen eighty-six as the

101
00:03:21,527 --> 00:03:23,496
California Perfume Company,

102
00:03:23,496 --> 00:03:26,268
founded by a book salesman named David

103
00:03:26,268 --> 00:03:29,258
McConnell, who gave away perfume to women

104
00:03:29,258 --> 00:03:31,009
who let him in the door.

105
00:03:31,189 --> 00:03:33,450
He discovered they liked the perfume more

106
00:03:33,450 --> 00:03:34,277
than the books.

107
00:03:34,457 --> 00:03:37,238
So he started selling perfume instead,

108
00:03:37,238 --> 00:03:40,239
using women as his sales representatives.

109
00:03:40,588 --> 00:03:42,947
By the fifties and sixties,

110
00:03:42,947 --> 00:03:44,431
Avon was a giant.

111
00:03:44,611 --> 00:03:48,364
Its famous television ads featured a doorbell,

112
00:03:48,364 --> 00:03:50,567
and the words Avon calling.

113
00:03:50,747 --> 00:03:53,828
Avon ladies were usually housewives from the

114
00:03:53,828 --> 00:03:55,999
neighborhood, selling lipstick,

115
00:03:55,999 --> 00:03:58,869
perfume, lotion and soap to their friends

116
00:03:58,869 --> 00:03:59,850
and neighbors.

117
00:04:00,030 --> 00:04:02,759
They carried a little case of samples

118
00:04:02,759 --> 00:04:03,791
and a catalog.

119
00:04:04,141 --> 00:04:06,357
For many women in those years,

120
00:04:06,357 --> 00:04:08,868
being an Avon lady was their first

121
00:04:08,868 --> 00:04:10,419
job outside the home,

122
00:04:10,419 --> 00:04:12,487
or a way to earn money while

123
00:04:12,487 --> 00:04:13,743
raising children.

124
00:04:13,923 --> 00:04:15,530
They set their own hours,

125
00:04:15,530 --> 00:04:17,266
kept a share of every sale,

126
00:04:17,266 --> 00:04:19,259
and became part of a network of

127
00:04:19,259 --> 00:04:20,866
women across the country.

128
00:04:21,046 --> 00:04:22,380
By the seventies,

129
00:04:22,380 --> 00:04:26,305
Avon had hundreds of thousands of representatives.

130
00:04:26,655 --> 00:04:29,174
Children remember the Avon lady well.

131
00:04:29,354 --> 00:04:32,165
She often left little lipstick samples,

132
00:04:32,165 --> 00:04:34,544
and those famous novelty bottles.

133
00:04:34,724 --> 00:04:37,344
Cologne in the shape of a car,

134
00:04:37,344 --> 00:04:39,703
a candle, a duck or a chess

135
00:04:39,703 --> 00:04:40,227
piece.

136
00:04:40,407 --> 00:04:43,305
Many grandfathers had a shelf full of

137
00:04:43,305 --> 00:04:47,142
Avon aftershave bottles shaped like antique cars,

138
00:04:47,142 --> 00:04:50,589
and those bottles are now collector's items.

139
00:04:50,939 --> 00:04:53,293
The secret of the Avon lady was

140
00:04:53,293 --> 00:04:53,748
trust.

141
00:04:53,928 --> 00:04:55,507
She wasn't a stranger.

142
00:04:55,687 --> 00:04:57,831
She was the woman from the PTA,

143
00:04:57,831 --> 00:05:00,389
from church, or from down the street.

144
00:05:00,569 --> 00:05:02,145
She knew your skin tone,

145
00:05:02,145 --> 00:05:04,772
your favorite perfume and your birthday.

146
00:05:04,952 --> 00:05:07,946
Her customers bought from her for years,

147
00:05:07,946 --> 00:05:09,294
sometimes decades.

148
00:05:09,474 --> 00:05:10,627
It was business,

149
00:05:10,627 --> 00:05:12,574
but it was also friendship.

150
00:05:12,923 --> 00:05:15,965
Then there was the encyclopedia salesman.

151
00:05:16,145 --> 00:05:18,018
He usually came in the evening,

152
00:05:18,018 --> 00:05:19,710
when both parents were home.

153
00:05:19,890 --> 00:05:22,008
He sat on the living room couch,

154
00:05:22,008 --> 00:05:24,193
opened a beautiful sample volume,

155
00:05:24,193 --> 00:05:26,774
and talked about the children's future.

156
00:05:26,954 --> 00:05:28,398
Did you want your kids to do

157
00:05:28,398 --> 00:05:29,172
well in school?

158
00:05:29,352 --> 00:05:30,594
To go to college?

159
00:05:30,774 --> 00:05:33,015
Then they needed the encyclopedia.

160
00:05:33,364 --> 00:05:35,470
A full set of World Book or

161
00:05:35,470 --> 00:05:38,277
Britannica cost hundreds of dollars,

162
00:05:38,277 --> 00:05:40,149
a big sum at a time when

163
00:05:40,149 --> 00:05:42,878
a family might earn five thousand a

164
00:05:42,878 --> 00:05:43,268
year.

165
00:05:43,448 --> 00:05:45,948
So salesmen offered payment plans,

166
00:05:45,948 --> 00:05:48,301
a few dollars a month for years.

167
00:05:48,481 --> 00:05:50,902
Parents signed, feeling they were doing the

168
00:05:50,902 --> 00:05:52,591
best thing for their children.

169
00:05:52,771 --> 00:05:54,977
Some sets were never paid off.

170
00:05:55,326 --> 00:05:59,405
Those encyclopedias sat on shelves in millions

171
00:05:59,405 --> 00:06:00,203
of homes.

172
00:06:00,383 --> 00:06:03,265
Children used them for school reports,

173
00:06:03,265 --> 00:06:05,920
copying paragraphs about volcanoes,

174
00:06:05,920 --> 00:06:08,347
presidents and the solar system.

175
00:06:08,527 --> 00:06:11,881
Some families bought the yearly update volume,

176
00:06:11,881 --> 00:06:12,173
too.

177
00:06:12,353 --> 00:06:15,175
And many people still remember the smell

178
00:06:15,175 --> 00:06:17,715
of the pages and the beautiful color

179
00:06:17,715 --> 00:06:19,690
pictures of birds and flags.

180
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:23,600
Encyclopedia salesmen were famous for their persistence,

181
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:24,998
and for their pitches.

182
00:06:25,178 --> 00:06:28,429
Many were college students working summer jobs,

183
00:06:28,429 --> 00:06:30,988
trained to memorize every word of the

184
00:06:30,988 --> 00:06:31,749
sales talk.

185
00:06:31,929 --> 00:06:34,415
They traveled from town to town in

186
00:06:34,415 --> 00:06:37,266
crews, sometimes driven around in a car

187
00:06:37,266 --> 00:06:38,582
by a crew manager.

188
00:06:38,762 --> 00:06:40,480
Some loved the work.

189
00:06:40,660 --> 00:06:42,227
Many quit after a week.

190
00:06:42,578 --> 00:06:44,540
And there was the vacuum cleaner man.

191
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:47,734
Electrolux and Kirby sold their machines door

192
00:06:47,734 --> 00:06:50,347
to door, and their salesmen were famous

193
00:06:50,347 --> 00:06:52,022
for their demonstrations.

194
00:06:52,202 --> 00:06:54,480
A Kirby man might sprinkle dirt on

195
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:56,556
your carpet, then vacuum it up.

196
00:06:56,736 --> 00:06:59,066
Then he'd show you a white filter,

197
00:06:59,066 --> 00:07:01,122
full of dust from a carpet you

198
00:07:01,122 --> 00:07:02,355
thought was clean.

199
00:07:02,705 --> 00:07:04,795
Those machines were expensive,

200
00:07:04,795 --> 00:07:07,164
often costing hundreds of dollars.

201
00:07:07,344 --> 00:07:09,039
But they were built to last.

202
00:07:09,219 --> 00:07:12,603
Many families used the same Electrolux or

203
00:07:12,603 --> 00:07:15,244
Kirby for thirty or forty years.

204
00:07:15,424 --> 00:07:18,434
And the salesman often came back years

205
00:07:18,434 --> 00:07:20,810
later to sell the family bags,

206
00:07:20,810 --> 00:07:22,553
belts and attachments.

207
00:07:22,902 --> 00:07:26,200
Some salesmen sold even bigger things.

208
00:07:26,380 --> 00:07:28,396
Sewing machines from Singer,

209
00:07:28,396 --> 00:07:30,988
often sold on installment plans of a

210
00:07:30,988 --> 00:07:32,429
few dollars a month.

211
00:07:32,609 --> 00:07:33,909
Water softeners.

212
00:07:34,089 --> 00:07:36,905
Aluminum siding, which could cover an old

213
00:07:36,905 --> 00:07:38,210
house in a weekend.

214
00:07:38,390 --> 00:07:40,631
Storm windows and doors.

215
00:07:40,811 --> 00:07:42,484
And cemetery plots,

216
00:07:42,484 --> 00:07:45,652
sold by men with very serious faces.

217
00:07:45,832 --> 00:07:47,786
If it could be carried up a

218
00:07:47,786 --> 00:07:50,245
porch step or shown in a brochure,

219
00:07:50,245 --> 00:07:52,415
somebody sold it door to door.

220
00:07:52,765 --> 00:07:54,911
In the fifties and sixties,

221
00:07:54,911 --> 00:07:57,375
the doorbell rang all day long.

222
00:07:57,555 --> 00:07:59,411
The milkman delivered milk,

223
00:07:59,411 --> 00:08:01,955
butter and eggs in the early morning.

224
00:08:02,135 --> 00:08:04,581
The bread man came with fresh bread

225
00:08:04,581 --> 00:08:05,560
and doughnuts.

226
00:08:05,740 --> 00:08:08,982
The Charles Chips truck brought potato chips

227
00:08:08,982 --> 00:08:11,266
and pretzels in big metal tins.

228
00:08:11,446 --> 00:08:14,606
And the Schwan's man delivered ice cream

229
00:08:14,606 --> 00:08:15,870
and frozen food.

230
00:08:16,220 --> 00:08:17,875
There were insurance men,

231
00:08:17,875 --> 00:08:20,259
who came every week to collect small

232
00:08:20,259 --> 00:08:21,385
premiums in cash,

233
00:08:21,385 --> 00:08:23,371
and wrote it in a little book.

234
00:08:23,551 --> 00:08:25,535
There were Bible salesmen,

235
00:08:25,535 --> 00:08:28,358
cookware salesmen and knife salesmen.

236
00:08:28,538 --> 00:08:31,082
There were Girl Scouts with cookies and

237
00:08:31,082 --> 00:08:32,648
Boy Scouts with popcorn.

238
00:08:32,828 --> 00:08:34,906
And there was the Good Humor man,

239
00:08:34,906 --> 00:08:37,174
ringing his bells down the street on

240
00:08:37,174 --> 00:08:38,308
summer afternoons.

241
00:08:38,658 --> 00:08:40,863
Kids sold door to door too.

242
00:08:41,043 --> 00:08:45,312
Seeds, greeting cards and magazine subscriptions,

243
00:08:45,312 --> 00:08:47,839
from ads in the back of comic

244
00:08:47,839 --> 00:08:50,888
books, for prizes like a bicycle or

245
00:08:50,888 --> 00:08:51,934
a telescope.

246
00:08:52,114 --> 00:08:54,249
Most kids sold just enough to get

247
00:08:54,249 --> 00:08:55,155
a cheap prize.

248
00:08:55,335 --> 00:08:57,103
But going door to door taught a

249
00:08:57,103 --> 00:08:59,384
whole generation of children how to talk

250
00:08:59,384 --> 00:09:01,779
to strangers, and how to handle rejection.

251
00:09:02,129 --> 00:09:04,282
And there was the Jewel Tea man,

252
00:09:04,282 --> 00:09:06,705
the Watkins man and the Raleigh man,

253
00:09:06,705 --> 00:09:09,935
who drove routes through the countryside selling

254
00:09:09,935 --> 00:09:13,030
spices, vanilla, liniment and household goods.

255
00:09:13,210 --> 00:09:14,389
For farm families,

256
00:09:14,389 --> 00:09:16,484
those route men were a lifeline.

257
00:09:16,664 --> 00:09:18,882
They often stayed for coffee,

258
00:09:18,882 --> 00:09:21,635
brought news from neighboring farms,

259
00:09:21,635 --> 00:09:24,693
and sometimes even took eggs or chickens

260
00:09:24,693 --> 00:09:25,534
as payment.

261
00:09:25,885 --> 00:09:27,337
In the late forties,

262
00:09:27,337 --> 00:09:29,298
a new way of selling began.

263
00:09:29,478 --> 00:09:30,836
The home party.

264
00:09:31,016 --> 00:09:35,213
Tupperware, the plastic food containers invented by

265
00:09:35,213 --> 00:09:38,505
Earl Tupper, didn't sell well in stores.

266
00:09:38,685 --> 00:09:41,919
People didn't understand how the airtight seal

267
00:09:41,919 --> 00:09:42,411
worked.

268
00:09:42,591 --> 00:09:45,787
So a saleswoman named Brownie Wise suggested

269
00:09:45,787 --> 00:09:49,128
demonstrating it at parties in people's homes.

270
00:09:49,478 --> 00:09:52,754
A hostess invited her friends over for

271
00:09:52,754 --> 00:09:54,133
coffee and cake.

272
00:09:54,313 --> 00:09:57,435
The Tupperware dealer showed off the products,

273
00:09:57,435 --> 00:10:00,217
burping the lids to demonstrate the seal,

274
00:10:00,217 --> 00:10:01,778
and played party games.

275
00:10:01,958 --> 00:10:03,288
Guests placed orders,

276
00:10:03,288 --> 00:10:05,314
and the hostess got a free gift.

277
00:10:05,494 --> 00:10:08,616
Tupperware parties became a huge social event

278
00:10:08,616 --> 00:10:11,183
for women in the fifties and sixties.

279
00:10:11,533 --> 00:10:14,013
Brownie Wise became one of the most

280
00:10:14,013 --> 00:10:16,281
famous businesswomen in America.

281
00:10:16,461 --> 00:10:18,198
In nineteen fifty-four,

282
00:10:18,198 --> 00:10:20,539
she was the first woman ever to

283
00:10:20,539 --> 00:10:23,258
appear on the cover of Business Week

284
00:10:23,258 --> 00:10:23,938
magazine.

285
00:10:24,118 --> 00:10:26,453
And the home party model spread to

286
00:10:26,453 --> 00:10:29,749
cosmetics, candles, kitchen tools and much more.

287
00:10:29,929 --> 00:10:30,680
In a way,

288
00:10:30,680 --> 00:10:32,518
it still exists today.

289
00:10:32,868 --> 00:10:35,027
Other companies copied the idea.

290
00:10:35,207 --> 00:10:38,094
Stanley Home Products had been holding parties

291
00:10:38,094 --> 00:10:39,538
